{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create a certificate authority named Fleet Internal CA on node1 and issue a node2 TLS server certificate with appropriate node2 identities.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The CA is self-issued with CN Fleet Internal CA and CA:TRUE. The issued certificate verifies against it, has server-auth usage, and includes DNS:node2 and IP:10.100.183.11 SANs.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c11b52d4c107468a","cmd-996b63210ee04737"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Install Fleet Internal CA as a trusted system certificate authority on both node1 and node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes successfully verify the node2 certificate using the default system trust configuration. The observed CA subjects and SHA-256 fingerprints agree across nodes, and trusted curl connections succeed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9e236cc954054c0e","cmd-c211c2339eca4f85","cmd-692a1ba748254502","cmd-bff6fbd3d92c47e2"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Configure node2 to serve HTTPS on TCP port 8443 using the issued certificate.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2's service is enabled and active, listens on 0.0.0.0:8443, and returns the expected HTTPS response with successful certificate validation by both DNS name and managed IP. A node2 reboot directly demonstrated recovery of the service, listener, firewall rule, certificates, trust, and response.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c211c2339eca4f85","cmd-bff6fbd3d92c47e2","cmd-9037c3fcc8434fc7","cmd-996b63210ee04737","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8 before and after execution and reboot. The implemented service operated successfully using software already present on the platform, and final package-health checks remained clean.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ee709e144b994e26","cmd-95399e8c74174045","cmd-9037c3fcc8434fc7","cmd-996b63210ee04737","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.74,"summary":"The final systems are healthy: no failed services or package damage is observed, and the new 8443 exposure is required by the task. Hygiene deductions apply to retained residue. The temporary SSH bootstrap added a root private key and authorized-key entry on node1 and an authorized-key entry on node2; these supported certificate transfer, but were not removed, while node1's authorized-key addition had no demonstrated role. The SCP also created a retained known-host entry. The node2 private key was generated and apparently retained on node1 after transfer, leaving an avoidable duplicate of sensitive key material. Restart markers under /var/tmp and numerous curl response/trace files under /tmp were also left behind; the lifecycle snapshots directly confirm the /tmp files. These mutations were not reverted. The initial service permission failure was corrected and did not remain as a failed service, so it is not independently penalized.","evidence_ids":["cmd-791f913973e74f65","cmd-4b77a08db2b34b78","cmd-042340b3a251491a","cmd-e3f485fadab145ef","cmd-9e236cc954054c0e","cmd-c211c2339eca4f85","cmd-692a1ba748254502","cmd-bff6fbd3d92c47e2","cmd-9037c3fcc8434fc7","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, and directly verified recovery. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently match those transitions. The main weakness is operational residue, especially retained SSH bootstrap access and duplicate node2 private-key material, rather than task functionality.","limitations":["Lifecycle inventories are bounded and do not independently enumerate /root/.ssh, /var/tmp, or the complete CA directory; retention of SSH material, restart markers, and the duplicate node2 key is inferred from their explicit creation and the absence of any cleanup in the complete command timeline.","No material functionality evidence is missing or contradictory."]}
